{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.9,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["R36"],
      "pins": ["R36.1", "R36.2"],
      "nets": ["VOUTN", "VOUTP"],
      "observed": "R36 has schematic value \"24k\" but its MPN FRC0805J240TSD and description state 24Ω. The two fields disagree by a factor of 1000.",
      "expected": "The value and sourcing metadata should agree. In this bleeder position across VOUTP/VOUTN, 24k is the electrically sensible value; a 24Ω part would impose an excessive load if PoE voltage is present.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(comp (ref \"R36\") (value \"24k\") ... (description \" 125mW Thick Film Resistors 150V ±100ppm/℃ ±5% 24Ω 0805 Chip Resistor - Surface Mount ROHS\") ... (MPN \"FRC0805J240TSD\")"},
        {"source": "spice", "quote": "R36 VOUTN VOUTP 24k"}
      ],
      "impact": "If assembly follows the MPN/LCSC field, a 24Ω resistor will be fitted across the PoE extraction rail instead of 24k, causing a very large load and possible overheating; if the value field is used, the BOM metadata is incorrect.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["D30", "R60"],
      "pins": ["D30.2", "D30.4", "R60.1", "R60.2"],
      "nets": ["ESTOP_OUT", "Net-(D30-K2)"],
      "observed": "D30 pin 4 (A2) is tied directly to ESTOP_OUT, while D30 pin 2 (K2) is connected through R60 back to the same ESTOP_OUT net. The second LED has both terminals on the same net through its series resistor.",
      "expected": "The second LED should have its anode/cathode connected between ESTOP_OUT and an opposite rail (typically GND) so that it can indicate the e-stop state.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(node (ref \"D30\") (pin \"4\") (pinfunction \"A2\") (pintype \"input\")) in net ESTOP_OUT"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(node (ref \"R60\") (pin \"2\") (pintype \"passive\")) in net ESTOP_OUT"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"128\") (name \"Net-(D30-K2)\") ... (node (ref \"D30\") (pin \"2\") (pinfunction \"K2\") (pintype \"input\")) ... (node (ref \"R60\") (pin \"1\") (pintype \"passive\")))"}
      ],
      "impact": "No potential difference ever appears across the second LED, so it will never illuminate. The board loses the intended e-stop status indication from D30.",
      "confidence": 0.9
    },
    {
      "category": "open_connection",
      "components": ["D4", "D14", "D23"],
      "pins": ["D4.2", "D14.2", "D23.2"],
      "nets": ["LED_3.3V_POWER_SW"],
      "observed": "The net LED_3.3V_POWER_SW contains only the anodes of D4, D14, and D23. There is no connection to any supply rail, driver, jumper, or other source on this net.",
      "expected": "LED anodes must be connected to a 3.3V supply or switched LED supply so that the LEDs can conduct when their cathodes are pulled low by the Ethernet/activity signals.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"32\") (name \"LED_3.3V_POWER_SW\") (class \"Default\") (node (ref \"D14\") (pin \"2\") (pinfunction \"A\") (pintype \"passive\")) (node (ref \"D23\") (pin \"2\") (pinfunction \"A\") (pintype \"passive\")) (node (ref \"D4\") (pin \"2\") (pinfunction \"A\") (pintype \"passive\")))"}
      ],
      "impact": "The three LEDs D4, D14, and D23 have floating anodes and will not light, so Ethernet link/activity and related status indications are nonfunctional.",
      "confidence": 0.85
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The design files contain several concrete deviations: R36 has conflicting 24k/24Ω metadata, D30's second LED is wired back to its own anode net through R60 so it can never light, and the D4/D14/D23 LED anodes sit on an undriven net. These would cause assembly and functional differences from the intended board."
}

